# Lessons Learned and Troubleshooting

## UTM on Apple Silicon

### Virtualize vs. Emulate

| Mode | Speed | Compatibility | Use Case |
|---|---|---|---|
| Virtualize | Fast | ARM64 guests only | Ubuntu ARM64, Windows ARM64 |
| Emulate | Slow | x86 guests possible | pfSense, Windows x86 (not recommended) |

**Lesson:** Always use Virtualize with ARM64 images on M1 Macs.

### Network Troubleshooting

| Problem | Solution |
|---|---|
| VM has no IP | Confirm Shared Network mode, restart VM |
| Can ping 8.8.8.8 but not google.com | DNS issue; check /etc/resolv.conf |
| Cannot reach Ubuntu mirrors | Switch to HTTPS sources, try different network |

## Ubuntu Package Management

### HTTP Mirror Failures

**Symptom:** `apt update` fails with connection errors to archive.ubuntu.com

**Fix:**

```bash
sudo sed -i 's|http://|https://|g' /etc/apt/sources.list
sudo sed -i 's|http://|https://|g' /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.sources
sudo apt clean
sudo apt update
```

### Disk Space Warnings

**Symptom:** Installations fail, services won't start

**Check:**

```bash
df -h
```

**Wazuh minimum:** 60 GB recommended for SOC-SIEM VM

## Windows Endpoint Specific

### Windows ARM Image Won't Boot

**Symptom:** Black screen or boot loop after import

**Solutions:**
- Use the UTM Windows installer wizard (downloads correct image)
- Ensure you imported a VHDX, not an ISO, for ARM Windows
- Allocate at least 2 GB RAM and 2 CPU cores

### Wazuh Agent Not Connecting on Windows

**Checklist:**

```powershell
# Verify service running
Get-Service WazuhSvc

# Check manager IP in config
notepad "C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\ossec.conf"

# Test connectivity to SIEM
Test-NetConnection -ComputerName 192.168.64.5 -Port 1514

# Restart agent
Restart-Service WazuhSvc
```

### Sysmon Not Collecting Events

**Symptom:** No Sysmon events in Wazuh

**Fix:**

```powershell
# Verify Sysmon running
Get-Service Sysmon64

# Check Sysmon log
Get-EventLog -LogName "Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational" -Newest 5

# Reinstall if needed
.\sysmon64.exe -u
.\sysmon64.exe -accepteula -i sysmonconfig.xml
```

### PowerShell Logs Not in Wazuh

**Symptom:** PowerShell commands execute but no alerts

**Fix:**
1. Open `gpedit.msc`
2. Enable Script Block Logging, Module Logging, and Transcription
3. Run `gpupdate /force`
4. Restart Wazuh service: `Restart-Service WazuhSvc`

### Windows VM Slow Performance

| Action | Impact |
|---|---|
| Reduce RAM to 2 GB | Significant improvement |
| Disable Windows visual effects | Moderate improvement |
| Shut down Linux-Endpoint | Frees 1-1.5 GB |
| Disable Windows Defender real-time (lab only) | Large improvement but disables IR-009 |

## Wazuh Specific

### Dashboard Certificate Warning

**Symptom:** Browser shows certificate error

**Solution:** Normal for lab environments. Accept the self-signed certificate and proceed.

### Agent Not Appearing

| Check | Linux Command | Windows Command |
|---|---|---|
| Agent service running? | `sudo systemctl status wazuh-agent` | `Get-Service WazuhSvc` |
| Correct manager IP? | Check `/var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf` | Check `ossec.conf` in Program Files |
| Firewall blocking? | Check UFW on SIEM | Check Windows Firewall |
| Network reachable? | `ping SOC-SIEM-IP` | `ping SOC-SIEM-IP` |
| Port 1514 open on SIEM? | `sudo ufw allow 1514` | N/A (SIEM-side) |

### Wazuh Install Script Missing

**Symptom:** `No such file or directory` when running installer

**Fix:**

```bash
cd ~
curl -O https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/wazuh-install.sh
ls -lh wazuh-install.sh
sudo bash ./wazuh-install.sh -a
```

## Resource Management on 8 GB RAM

### Running Combination Guide

| Scenario | VMs to Run | Total RAM Used |
|---|---|---|
| Wazuh setup | SOC-SIEM only | 4 GB |
| Linux attack lab | SOC-SIEM + Linux + Attacker | 6-7 GB |
| Windows attack lab | SOC-SIEM + Windows + Attacker | 7-8 GB |
| Cross-platform review | SOC-SIEM + Linux + Windows | 7-8 GB (very tight) |

**Lesson:** Run only ONE endpoint platform (Linux OR Windows) alongside the SIEM and attacker at a time.

